# Object storage

## Containers

`nemau_container` manages the container but not its contents. Upload objects
with the CLI or an SDK.

`object_lock` and `retention_mode` force a replacement, because S3 cannot add
object locking to an existing bucket. A container meant to hold immutable
backups has to be created with object lock enabled. See the
[immutable backups example](https://github.com/nemau-cloud/terraform-provider-nemau/tree/main/examples/immutable-backups).

`versioning` changes in place, but cannot be turned off while `object_lock` is
on. `retention_days` can only be increased in place. Shortening the retention
of a WORM container is a support operation that the API does not offer, and
the plan refuses it. After an update, the state records what the object store
reports, so a change the platform did not apply is reported as an error.

The computed `immutable` attribute is read from the object store rather than
derived from the configuration, so it shows what an audit would observe.

Under `COMPLIANCE`, `terraform destroy` fails while locked object versions
remain. The platform cannot delete them either.

## S3 access keys

`nemau_container_credential` creates a read-only or read-write key scoped to
one `nemau_container`. The platform returns the secret once and cannot recover
it, so the Terraform state holds the only copy. Use an encrypted remote backend
and restrict who can read the state. Creating and revoking keys requires
permission to manage storage credentials (`storage:ManageCredentials`).

S3 tools address the key's `s3_bucket` (also available as
`nemau_container.<name>.s3_bucket`) rather than the container name, because the
object store knows each container under an opaque bucket name. See the
[container credential example](https://github.com/nemau-cloud/terraform-provider-nemau/tree/main/examples/container-credential).
